## Reproduced boundary

Low-priority path-fidelity edge case on main `eac19c5bf3ceb7a82f1e958ac97bef4d9af16fc6`, Node v24.19.0/Linux and the exact committed SDK `2026.10.13` dependency graph.

A valid Git repository whose directory name ends with a space cannot use `--changed`. The new [file-focus root discovery](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/blob/eac19c5bf3ceb7a82f1e958ac97bef4d9af16fc6/file-focus.ts#L65-L86) calls `.trim()` on `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, deleting the actual final character of the directory name. Subsequent Git commands run against a different/nonexistent path and the error incorrectly recommends fetching a known base.

The [documented goal](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/blob/eac19c5bf3ceb7a82f1e958ac97bef4d9af16fc6/README.md#L56-L86) is file focus from a real Git working repository with actionable errors for invalid repositories/bases. Here the repository and its main commit are both valid.

## Small real-Git reproduction

Run from the reviewed package after installing its locked dependencies:

```js
import { mkdtempSync, mkdirSync, writeFileSync, renameSync, rmSync } from "node:fs";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { execFileSync } from "node:child_process";
import { resolveFileFocus } from "./dist/file-focus.js";

const parent = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "focus-space-"));
const root = join(parent, "project ");
mkdirSync(root);
const git = (...args) => execFileSync("git", [
  "-c", "user.name=Fixture", "-c", "user.email=<fixture address>", ...args,
], { cwd: root, encoding: "utf8" });

try {
  git("init", "-b", "main");
  writeFileSync(join(root, "file.ts"), "initial");
  git("add", ".");
  git("commit", "-m", "Base");
  writeFileSync(join(root, "file.ts"), "changed");

  console.log(JSON.stringify(git("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")));
  try {
    console.log(resolveFileFocus({ changed: true }, [], root, join(root, ".agents/pm")));
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error.code, error.exitCode);
  }

  const normal = join(parent, "project");
  renameSync(root, normal);
  console.log(resolveFileFocus({ changed: true }, [], normal, join(normal, ".agents/pm")));
} finally {
  rmSync(parent, { recursive: true, force: true });
}
```

Actual:
- Git reports the correct root, ending in `"project \\n"`
- Space-suffixed root: `UNKNOWN_BASE`, exit code 2
- Rename the same repository to `project`, without changing its contents/history: succeeds with `files: ["file.ts"]`

The direct helper failure proves pm-context itself loses the path even when given the correct cwd. A second end-to-end control used a real SDK-initialized tracker with the built extension: ordinary `context-pack --format json` succeeded in the space-suffixed directory, but `context-pack --changed main --format json` exited 2 with “File focus requires a Git working repository.” That host path can already be normalized before reaching the helper, so fixing this helper alone should not be assumed to close the installed-CLI case.

## Expected and scope

Preserve the exact repository root returned by Git, removing only Git's terminating newline rather than meaningful filename whitespace. The same working repository should select the same changed file before and after the directory rename.

This is a narrow Unix path edge case, not a general failure of normal paths or spaces inside names. No tracker corruption or data loss was observed. The 17 authored file-focus tests pass on ordinary roots.

## Acceptance

- Real Git regression with a trailing-space repository directory
- Same-repository rename control with unchanged history and contents
- Public helper and installed-CLI paths both work, or clearly separate any required host-side correction
- Continue rejecting truly missing repositories and unknown bases

## Duplicate check

Reviewed all 152 pre-existing GitHub issues/PRs across all states plus current canonical PM issues/features; no matching path-fidelity defect report was present. The related feature owner is [pm-context-ax7z](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/blob/eac19c5bf3ceb7a82f1e958ac97bef4d9af16fc6/.agents/pm/features/pm-context-ax7z.toon), delivered through [PR #151](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-context/pull/151). Issue #153 is the separately reproduced overlap-packing defect. No implementation changes or dependency-pin changes were made.


